 ## Background
-Regularization in NeRFs emerged as a response to the "floater" artifacts and noise inherent in learning radiance fields from sparse or unstructured data. Early works like [S1] (Simple-RF) and [S6] (SGCNeRF) introduced occlusion and density-based regularizations to suppress artifacts near the camera. Subsequent works like [S2] (RING-NeRF) and [S3] (SimpleNeRF) refined these with coarse-to-fine consistency and black/white priors. The use of SH regularization [S1, S2] and normal consistency [S4] addresses view-dependent appearance and geometric smoothness, respectively. MixNeRF [S5] introduced probabilistic modeling for sparse inputs. These techniques are widely used in few-shot novel view synthesis, sparse-view reconstruction, and low-light rendering.
+Regularization in NeRFs emerged as a response to the inherent ill-posedness of the inverse rendering problem, particularly when training data is sparse or noisy. Early NeRF implementations often produced "floaters" (spurious geometry in empty space) or blurry reconstructions. Simple-RF [S1] and SimpleNeRF [S3] introduced simplified augmentation strategies and consistency losses to address these issues in sparse settings. RING-NeRF [S2] highlighted the importance of density losses combined with coarse-to-fine sampling for stability. FreeNeRF [S6] formalized occlusion regularization to suppress near-camera artifacts. Ref-NeRF [S4] and subsequent works like NeRF-Casting introduced normal-based regularization to improve surface detail and specular consistency. MixNeRF [S5] utilized mixture density models to handle ambiguity in sparse inputs. These techniques collectively form the backbone of robust NeRF training configurations.
 
